strategems/ — reusable dataflow patterns proven useful across many areas.

A strategem is a shape of data flow that keeps working no matter where you point
it: the same pattern that routes a spell's aim also routes an NCP's aim; the
same "configure the template, never the instantiation" pattern governs both the
NPC inventory UI and the settlement's worker shops. When a pattern earns its keep
in more than one place, write it down here so the next area can reuse it instead
of reinventing it.

Write here whenever a pattern proves itself twice. Re-read at random — a
strategem from the economy might be exactly what the Dungeon Master needs.

Seed observations (patterns already visible in the vision, to grow into full
strategems as they prove out):
- **Aim once, aim everywhere.** One input abstraction layer that spells, NCPs,
  and the player all aim through — the source of the motion changes, the routing
  does not.
- **Configure templates, never instantiations.** The NPC inventory UI and the
  settlement worker allocation are the same pattern: you edit the mold, the world
  stamps the copies.
- **Remember the demonstrated, re-estimate the meaning.** The Dungeon Master
  watches what a party actually does, then updates what a "level" means — a
  feedback loop that could tune difficulty, prices, or province relationships
  alike.
